\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb T=\partial\mathbb D$. Let $A$ be the boundary restrictions to $\mathbb T$ of the disc algebra: functions continuous on $\overline{\mathbb D}$ and analytic in $\mathbb D$. Let $\psi:\mathbb T\to\mathbb T$ be an orientation-reversing homeomorphism and set \[A_\psi=\{f\in C(\mathbb T):f\in A\text{ and }f\circ\psi\in A\}.\]
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Characterize the orientation-reversing homeomorphisms $\psi$ for which $A_\psi$ consists exactly of the constant functions. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
When can a circle function and its orientation-reversed reparametrization both lie in the disc algebra only if the function is constant?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2308025 (AMR-022-8025). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Walter K. Hayman and Edward F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory} (2018), arXiv:1809.07200. Problem 8.25, complete original question, all following updates, and the relevant chapter definitions. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
